description:
Location: workspace-wide. Grep of every `Cargo.toml` finds no `tracing-test`, `tracing-subscriber` (with `fmt::Layer` capture), `env_logger`, `slog-async-test`, or equivalent.

CONCERN
Today the workspace has zero log emission paths — no `tracing::*`, `log::*`, `info!`, `warn!`, `error!`, `debug!`, `trace!` call sites in any `crate-jmap-*/src/` file. The closed JMAP-sc1b.79 added a Debug-redaction canary test for BearerAuth and BasicAuth that asserts the token literal does not appear in `{:?}` output. That test catches static Debug-derive regressions on those two types.

It does NOT catch:
1. A future `tracing::debug!("sending auth: {}", auth_header)` call site that interpolates a secret-bearing argument via its `Display` impl rather than its `Debug` impl.
2. A new type that derives `Debug` with sensitive fields — only the two BearerAuth/BasicAuth tests guard, no general-purpose suite.
3. A `tracing::info!` call whose format string is benign but whose arguments cascade through to expose secret material (e.g. logging a whole Request whose body includes credentials).
4. The cases newly filed in this review: SpaceInvite, SpaceJoinInput, PushSubscriptionPatch, Session, WsFrame::Unknown.

When the workspace adds its first `tracing::*` instrumentation (any pre-1.0 production deployment will want some), the contributor has no scaffold to write a 'log line never contains the literal Bearer token value' assertion against. They will have to invent the harness from scratch and either skip the test or build a one-off.

WHAT GOES WRONG
Future logging-level secret leaks land unguarded. The risk is conditional on someone wiring tracing into the workspace — when that happens, there's no in-repo test pattern to copy.

PROPOSED FIX
1. Add a workspace-level `dev-dependencies.tracing-subscriber` with the `fmt` feature and a test-only helper crate or module that:

       struct LogCapture {
           buffer: Arc<Mutex<Vec<u8>>>,
           _guard: tracing::subscriber::DefaultGuard,
       }
       impl LogCapture {
           fn new() -> Self { ... }
           fn contents(&self) -> String { ... }
           fn assert_does_not_contain(&self, needle: &str) { ... }
       }

   Place it in a new crate `crate-jmap-test-support` or as a sibling module to the existing test helpers.

2. Write a representative-canary test using the harness:

       #[test]
       fn auth_token_never_appears_in_tracing_output() {
           let capture = LogCapture::new();
           let auth = BearerAuth::new("CANARY-LOG-TOKEN-DO-NOT-LEAK")?;
           // exercise every code path that touches auth (build a request, fail it, format it, etc.)
           ...
           capture.assert_does_not_contain("CANARY-LOG-TOKEN-DO-NOT-LEAK");
       }

3. Document the pattern in workspace AGENTS.md under a 'Security testing' subsection so future tracing additions land with a matching log-capture test.

ORACLE
- Closed JMAP-sc1b.79 — the type-level Debug canary precedent. This bead extends the same pattern to the log-capture level.
- `tracing-test` crate docs (https://docs.rs/tracing-test) — a third-party shortcut for this exact pattern, if rolling our own is too heavy.

RISK CLASSIFICATION
P3. Forward-looking gap. No live exposure; the bead exists to ensure the harness is in place before logging is added, not after.
